  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/corydoras-treitlii-2/",
      "html": "<p>The long-nosed Corydoras treitlii is one of the real rarities in the trade, although it is one of the longest known armored catfishes. It was scientifically described in 1906. With over 7 cm maximum length it belongs to the larger Corydoras species.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/corydoras-treitlii-2/",
      "html": "<p>Lower Amazon; specimens caught for export mostly come from the Belem area. There are duplicate species from the upper Amazonian drainage (Madre de Dios in Peru and Rio Purus in Brazil), the latter of which has been assigned C number 78.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "equipment",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/corydoras-treitlii-2/",
      "html": "<p>Offer the animals at least in places soft, fine sand as substrate.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/corydoras-treitlii-2/",
      "html": "<p>Genus-typical peaceful; it fits into every larger, well maintained community aquarium.</p>"
    }
  ],
